BackGround
Question
Spark currently implements the Delegation token provider for Hive.
At that time when a join was performed based on Spark reading data from Iceberg tables on two clusters,
which were associated with different HiveMetaStore，and Spark can only update the delegation token for one of the HiveMetaStore。
How to solve it
Based on Spark's Delegation token framework, implement the IcebergHiveConnectorDelegationTokenProvider in the Iceberg_Spark module with the following example
   import org.apache.spark.security.HadoopDelegationTokenProvider;
   public class IcebergHiveConnectorDelegationTokenProvider implements HadoopDelegationTokenProvider {

    @Override
    public String serviceName() {
        return "Iceberg_Hive";
    }

    @Override
    public boolean delegationTokensRequired(SparkConf sparkConf, Configuration hadoopConf) {
       ----Returns true if delegation tokens are required for this service.---
    }

    @Override
    public Option<Object> obtainDelegationTokens(Configuration hadoopConf, SparkConf sparkConf, Credentials creds) {
      -----Obtain delegation tokens for this service--
    }
}

The parameters involved in the current change are as follows
spark.sql.catalog.catalogName.uri = $HiveMetaStore_uri  (already existing)
spark.sql.catalog.catalogName.hive.metastore.kerberos.principal = $principal (additional)

After this pr, we can get the delegation token for the HiveMetaStore associated with the different catalogName on Iceberg
