How to solve it
Based on Flink's Delegation token framework, implement the IcebergHiveConnectorDelegationTokenProvider in the Iceberg_Flink module with the following example
import org.apache.flink.configuration.Configuration;
import org.apache.flink.core.security.token.DelegationTokenProvider;

public class IcebergHiveConnectorDelegationTokenProvider  implements DelegationTokenProvider {
    @Override
    public String serviceName() {
        return "Iceberg_Hive";
    }

    @Override
    public void init(Configuration configuration) throws Exception {

    }

    @Override
    public boolean delegationTokensRequired() throws Exception {
           ----Returns true if delegation tokens are required for this service.---
    }

    @Override
    public ObtainedDelegationTokens obtainDelegationTokens() throws Exception {
             -----Obtain delegation tokens for this service--
    }
}

When we create the following Hive Catalog through Flink to read iceberg data
CREATE CATALOG hive_catalog WITH (
  'type'='iceberg',
  'catalog-type'='hive',
  'uri'='thrift://localhost:9083',
  'clients'='5',
  'property-version'='1',
  'warehouse'='hdfs://nn:8020/warehouse/path'
);

The parameters involved in the current pr are add in hive_catalog as follows
catalog-type = hive (already existing)
uri = $HiveMetaStore_uri (already existing)
kerberos.principal = $principal (additional)

After this pr, we can get the delegation token for the HiveMetaStore associated with the different catalogName on Iceberg
